\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Fig. 1(a).—Velocity distribution of stars with fixed energy $E$;$J$ varies accordingly to the direction of $\mathbf  {v}$ emphasising the two-dimensional nature of the problem. The vertical axis points towards the compact object of mass $M$. Fig. 1(6).—The velocity sphere viewed from below. On the left the empty loss cone scenario; stellar density is nearly indipendent of $J$ everywhere but $J\gtrsim J_{min}$ where is quickly declines. On the right the full loss cone regime; consumed stars are immediately replenished by stars from a region much more extended- and uniform density- than the loss cone itself (the “pinhole” limit). Picture from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\textcite {Lightman_1977}.}}{18}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{loss cone image}{{1.2}{18}{Fig. 1(a).—Velocity distribution of stars with fixed energy $E$;$J$ varies accordingly to the direction of $\mathbf {v}$ emphasising the two-dimensional nature of the problem. The vertical axis points towards the compact object of mass $M$. Fig. 1(6).—The velocity sphere viewed from below. On the left the empty loss cone scenario; stellar density is nearly indipendent of $J$ everywhere but $J\gtrsim J_{min}$ where is quickly declines. On the right the full loss cone regime; consumed stars are immediately replenished by stars from a region much more extended- and uniform density- than the loss cone itself (the “pinhole” limit). Picture from \textcite {Lightman_1977}}{figure.caption.4}{}}
